    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Many property claim disputes are not about whether the damage is covered. They are about money. The insurer says your hail-damaged roof can be repaired for one figure, and your contractor says it needs replacement at three times that. <strong>Insurance appraisal in Dallas</strong> is a process built into many homeowners and commercial policies for exactly this kind of disagreement. It can be faster and less costly than litigation, but it is not right for every claim. Dallas Public Adjuster helps policyholders in Dallas County understand when appraisal fits, how to prepare, and what to expect.</p>

<h2>How the Appraisal Process Works</h2>
<p>Policy wording varies, but most appraisal clauses follow a similar pattern:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A dispute over the amount of loss.</strong> You and the insurer disagree on the cost to repair or replace covered damage.</li>
<li><strong>A written demand.</strong> Either side may demand appraisal in writing. Some policies have specific notice requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Each side selects an appraiser.</strong> Each party names a competent, impartial appraiser, often within a time limit stated in the policy.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers select an umpire.</strong> If they cannot agree on one, many policies allow a court to appoint the umpire.</li>
<li><strong>The appraisers evaluate the loss.</strong> They inspect, review estimates and documentation, and try to agree on the amount.</li>
<li><strong>The umpire resolves differences.</strong> Items the appraisers cannot agree on go to the umpire.</li>
<li><strong>The award.</strong> An award agreed to by any two of the three is generally binding on the amount of loss.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When Appraisal May Make Sense</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Appraisal a good fit?</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Insurer accepts hail damage but disagrees on repair vs. replacement</td><td>Often, since the dispute is about the scope and amount of loss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unit prices or quantities differ between estimates</td><td>Often</td></tr>
<tr><td>Disagreement over matching of shingles, siding, or flooring</td><td>Sometimes; depends on policy language and how the dispute is framed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full denial based on an exclusion</td><td>Usually not; that is a coverage dispute</td></tr>
<tr><td>Allegations of fraud or misrepresentation</td><td>No; consult a Texas attorney</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delay or claims-handling complaints</td><td>Usually not; appraisal sets the amount, not how the claim was handled</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Partial denials can be tricky. If the insurer accepted the roof but denied the interior, appraisal may settle the value of the roof while the interior remains a coverage question. Read your policy carefully, and see our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page for coverage disputes.</p>

<h2>Appraisal and Dallas Storm Claims</h2>
<p>Hail drives a large share of North Texas property disputes. NOAA has recorded 841 hail reports in Dallas County since 1955, peaking in April and May. Common appraisal issues on <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">Dallas hail damage claims</a> include whether a composition shingle roof should be repaired or replaced, how many slopes are damaged, whether metal accessories and gutters are included, and whether pricing reflects local labor costs after a major storm. Commercial appraisals often focus on low-slope membranes, wet insulation, and rooftop equipment.</p>

