<h2>Preparing for Appraisal</h2>
<p>An appraisal is only as strong as the documentation behind it. Before invoking the clause, it helps to have:</p>
<ul>
<li>A complete copy of your policy, including the declarations page and endorsements.</li>
<li>The insurer’s estimate, photos, and any engineering reports.</li>
<li>A detailed, line-item estimate of your own, such as an Xactimate estimate. See <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</li>
<li>Dated photos and video of the damage, ideally before temporary repairs.</li>
<li>Weather data for the date of loss. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> draws on NOAA records for Dallas County.</li>
<li>Contractor bids, material availability information, and any code requirements.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Costs and Tradeoffs</h2>
<p>Many policies say each side pays its own appraiser and shares the umpire’s costs. Appraisal can resolve disputes more quickly than litigation, but the award may come in higher or lower than you expected, and it is generally binding on the amount. The deductible, prior payments, depreciation terms, and policy limits still apply after the award. Weigh those factors before demanding appraisal.</p>

<h2>Our Role in the Appraisal Process</h2>
<p>We help policyholders decide whether appraisal is a sensible next step, prepare the documentation, and build a detailed estimate that supports their position. Joseph Dittman’s background includes work as a certified insurance appraiser and insurance umpire, as well as carrier-side and public adjusting, which helps in understanding how appraisal panels evaluate claims. Before appraisal, many disputes can still be narrowed through an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> or a reconsideration request.</p>
<p>Appraisal is not legal advice or litigation. If your situation involves coverage disputes, bad faith, or a deadline to sue, consult a Texas attorney. We cannot guarantee any appraisal result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. To talk through whether appraisal fits your claim, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
